  "notes": "West leads the ♠10 against 4♥. The defense cashes the first 2 spade tricks and exits a trump (a club shift would be better). When hearts prove to be 1-1, South has a certain method of establishing a diamond trick to take care of his club loser.\n\nThe ♦A should be cashed next. The closed hand is entered in trumps and the ♦9 is played. The defense will have no answer regardless of how their diamonds are divided. If West discards, a low diamond is played from dummy and East wins the ♦J. North's ♦Q and ♦10 can be used to take a ruffing finesse against East's ♦K.\n\nIf, on the play of the ♦9, West follows small, any diamond can be played from the dummy. East can win cheaply, revealing at worst a 4-2 diamond break. North still has sufficient entries to establish and cash a long diamond by ruffing.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♠10 against 4♥. The defense cashes the first 2 spade tricks and exits a trump (a club shift would be better). When hearts prove to be 1-1, South has a certain method of establishing a diamond trick to take care of his club loser.",
    "The ♦A should be cashed next. The closed hand is entered in trumps and the ♦9 is played. The defense will have no answer regardless of how their diamonds are divided. If West discards, a low diamond is played from dummy and East wins the ♦J. North's ♦Q and ♦10 can be used to take a ruffing finesse against East's ♦K.",
    "If, on the play of the ♦9, West follows small, any diamond can be played from the dummy. East can win cheaply, revealing at worst a 4-2 diamond break. North still has sufficient entries to establish and cash a long diamond by ruffing."
